{
  "concept": "The pressure–temperature phase diagram of water: the coexistence curves divide p–T space into solid, liquid, and vapor regions, so a substance's phase is fixed entirely by where its state point sits relative to those curves.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Title 'H2O' centered above the plot",
    "Vertical axis with arrowhead labeled 'Pressure, p (atm)' with nonlinear (quasi-logarithmic) ticks at 0.006, 1, and 218",
    "Horizontal axis with arrowhead labeled 'Temperature, T (°C)' with nonlinear ticks at 0, 0.01, 100, and 374",
    "Red fusion (melting) curve: rises nearly vertically from the triple point, leaning slightly LEFT (negative slope), off the top of the plot",
    "Red vaporization (boiling) curve: sweeps up and right from the triple point to the critical point",
    "Red sublimation curve: continues from the triple point down and left toward the origin corner",
    "Filled black dot at the triple point (0.01 °C, 0.006 atm) with label 'Triple point'",
    "Filled black dot at the critical point (374 °C, 218 atm) with label 'Critical point'",
    "Dashed guide lines from the triple point to both axes (to 0.006 atm and to 0.01 °C)",
    "Dashed guide lines from the critical point to both axes (to 218 atm and to 374 °C)",
    "Dashed guide lines marking the 1 atm / 100 °C crossing on the vaporization curve",
    "Italic region labels: 's' (solid, upper-left region), 'l' (liquid, wedge between melting and boiling curves), 'v' (vapor, lower-right region)",
    "Curved arrow annotations labeled 'Melting', 'Boiling', and 'Sublimation' pointing at the three red branches",
    "Movable state-point marker (open circle) with a live readout of T, p and current phase",
    "Highlight shading for whichever region the state point occupies",
    "Horizontal dashed path line with arrow for the selected heating path (1 atm, low-pressure, supercritical)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "temperature",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Moving horizontally across the diagram at fixed pressure crosses coexistence curves and changes the phase — the same substance is ice, water, or steam depending only on where it lands.",
      "label": "Temperature T (°C)",
      "range": [-20, 500, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "pressure",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Pressure alone can change the phase: raising p at fixed T can cross the melting curve (solid → liquid) or the vaporization curve (vapor → liquid).",
      "label": "Pressure p (atm)",
      "range": [0.001, 300, 0.001],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "heatingPath",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Which transitions a substance undergoes depends on the path through p–T space: at 1 atm ice melts then boils, below the triple-point pressure ice sublimates directly to vapor, and above the critical pressure no boundary is crossed at all.",
      "label": "Heating path",
      "options": ["Free explore", "1 atm path", "Low-pressure path (0.003 atm)", "Supercritical path (250 atm)"],
      "default": "Free explore"
    },
    {
      "id": "tracePath",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "Watching the state point sweep left to right along a fixed-pressure line makes the boundary crossings — and their absence above the critical point — appear as discrete events.",
      "label": "Heat the sample along the selected path",
      "loop": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the map",
      "narration": "Three red curves carve the plane into solid (s), liquid (l), and vapor (v). The state point sits at room conditions, 25 °C and 1 atm, inside the liquid wedge — water is liquid there for no other reason than its location on this map.",
      "state": { "temperature": 25, "pressure": 1, "heatingPath": "Free explore" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Cross the boiling curve",
      "narration": "Slide the temperature right at 1 atm. At 100 °C the point crosses the vaporization curve and the highlighted region flips from liquid to vapor — that crossing is boiling.",
      "state": { "temperature": 130, "pressure": 1, "heatingPath": "Free explore" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The triple point",
      "narration": "Drop the pressure to 0.006 atm and the temperature to 0.01 °C. All three curves meet at this single dot, the one condition where ice, water, and steam coexist.",
      "state": { "temperature": 0.01, "pressure": 0.006, "heatingPath": "Free explore" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Squeeze ice into water",
      "narration": "Park the temperature just below 0 °C in the solid region and raise the pressure. Because water's melting curve leans LEFT, climbing vertically eventually crosses it and the label changes from solid to liquid — pressure alone melts ice.",
      "state": { "temperature": -2, "pressure": 200, "heatingPath": "Free explore" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sublimation path",
      "narration": "Choose the low-pressure path, below the triple-point pressure, and heat. The point crosses only the sublimation curve: ice turns straight into vapor with no liquid stage.",
      "state": { "heatingPath": "Low-pressure path (0.003 atm)", "pressure": 0.003, "temperature": 20 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Past the critical point",
      "narration": "Now take the supercritical path above 218 atm and heat past 374 °C. No curve is crossed — liquid and vapor merge continuously into one indistinguishable supercritical fluid.",
      "state": { "heatingPath": "Supercritical path (250 atm)", "pressure": 250, "temperature": 450 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Phase isn't a property you carry with you — it's a location: the state point's position relative to the coexistence curves decides everything, which is why water's uniquely left-leaning melting line lets pressure alone turn ice into liquid.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single quadrant plot with arrow-tipped axes, white background, black axes and text, the three coexistence curves drawn as one continuous red stroke (~2px). Both axes are strongly nonlinear (log-like) so that 0.006, 1 and 218 atm, and 0, 0.01, 100 and 374 °C, are all comfortably separated; ticks are labeled only at those values. From the triple-point dot (low-left-center) one branch rises steeply and tilts slightly leftward toward the top of the frame (exaggerate the negative slope so it reads clearly), another sweeps up-and-right with increasing steepness to the critical-point dot at the upper right, and a third continues down-left to near the origin. Both special points are filled black dots with plain text labels beside them. Thin black dashed guides run from each special point and from the 1 atm/100 °C crossing to the axes. Italic single-letter region labels s (upper left), l (wedge between the two upper branches), v (lower right). Curved black arrows on the outside of the frame tag 'Melting' (left, pointing at the fusion curve), 'Boiling' (right, pointing at the vaporization curve), and 'Sublimation' (lower left). Interactive additions: a translucent fill tinting the currently occupied region (blue-grey for solid, blue for liquid, warm yellow for vapor, purple wash beyond the critical point), a prominent open-circle state marker with a small readout box showing T, p and the phase name, and when a heating path is selected a horizontal dashed arrow across the plot at that pressure with small tick marks and labels at each boundary crossing ('melting', 'boiling', 'sublimation', or 'no transition')."
}

